---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import DocsSidebar from "../../components/DocsSidebar.astro";
import CodeWindow from "../../components/CodeWindow.astro";
import PropsTable from "../../components/PropsTable.astro";
import JsApiDemo from "../../components/JsApiDemo.astro";
import "../../styles/docs.css";
---

<Layout title="JavaScript API — Motion Components" description="Trigger, pause, resume, finish, and cancel any motion component programmatically with a Web-Animations-style playback API." noMain>
  <div class="layout">
    <DocsSidebar />

    <main id="main">
      <h1><motion-split by="chars" interval="0.025" duration="0.5">JavaScript API</motion-split></h1>
      <p class="lead">Every animating component exposes a Web-Animations-style playback surface: <code>play()</code>, <code>pause()</code>, <code>finish()</code>, <code>cancel()</code>, a <code>playState</code> getter, and a <code>finished</code> promise. It also exposes package-level helpers to control everything at once.</p>

      <h2>Controlling playback</h2>
      <JsApiDemo target="api-ticker" wide>
        <motion-ticker id="api-ticker" speed="60">
          <span>motion-components</span>
        </motion-ticker>
      </JsApiDemo>
      <CodeWindow
        filename="app.ts"
        copy
        code={`const ticker = document.querySelector('motion-ticker')

ticker.pause()                  // freeze mid-phase
await ticker.play()             // resume; resolves when the run finishes
ticker.finish()                 // jump to the end state
ticker.cancel()                 // hard reset to the initial state

console.log(ticker.playState)   // 'idle' | 'running' | 'paused' | 'finished'`}
      />

      <h2>Triggering a one-shot and awaiting it</h2>
      <JsApiDemo target="api-reveal" mode="one-shot" wide>
        <motion-reveal id="api-reveal" duration="0.8" y="32">
          <div class="demo-card">Triggered from JavaScript</div>
        </motion-reveal>
      </JsApiDemo>
      <CodeWindow
        filename="app.ts"
        copy
        code={`const reveal = document.querySelector('motion-reveal')

reveal.cancel()
await reveal.play()
console.log('reveal complete')`}
      />

      <h2>Controlling everything at once</h2>
      <p>The package exports registry-backed helpers that operate on every live instance, or only those inside a container you pass. Input-driven components (<code>motion-hover</code>, <code>motion-press</code>, <code>motion-magnetic</code>, <code>motion-tilt</code>) have no timeline; <code>pauseAll()</code> disables them and <code>resumeAll()</code> re-enables only the ones it disabled.</p>
      <JsApiDemo target="pause-all-scope" mode="pause-all" wide>
        <div id="pause-all-scope">
          <div class="loop-row">
            <motion-circle text="orbit · orbit · orbit ·" duration="14"></motion-circle>
            <motion-typewriter loop>typing along…</motion-typewriter>
          </div>
        </div>
      </JsApiDemo>
      <CodeWindow
        filename="app.ts"
        copy
        code={`import { pauseAll, resumeAll } from 'motion-components'

document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', () => {
  document.hidden ? pauseAll() : resumeAll()
})

pauseAll(document.querySelector('#hero'))   // or scope to a subtree`}
      />

      <h2>Instance members</h2>
      <PropsTable
        nameLabel="Member"
        rows={[
          { name: "play()", type: "() => Promise<void>", default: "—", description: "Start if idle or finished, resume if paused, no-op if running" },
          { name: "pause()", type: "() => void", default: "—", description: "Freeze in place; resumable with play()" },
          { name: "finish()", type: "() => void", default: "—", description: "Jump to the end state immediately" },
          { name: "cancel()", type: "() => void", default: "—", description: "Hard terminate and reset to the initial state" },
          { name: "replay()", type: "() => void", default: "—", description: "Reset and run again from the start. On components with a one-shot animation, such as the reveals, text effects, charts, icons and <code>motion-code</code>" },
          { name: "playState", type: "PlaybackState", default: "'idle'", description: "'idle' | 'running' | 'paused' | 'finished'" },
          { name: "finished", type: "Promise<void>", default: "—", description: "Fresh per run; resolves on finish or cancel, never rejects" },
        ]}
      />

      <h2 id="events">Events</h2>
      <p>
        Every controllable component fires these events. They bubble and are composed, so one listener on a parent or on
        <code>document</code> hears every component inside it, including components inside another shadow root.
      </p>
      <PropsTable
        nameLabel="Event"
        rows={[
          { name: "motion-start", type: "CustomEvent", default: "—", description: "A run starts, from a trigger or from <code>play()</code>" },
          { name: "motion-finish", type: "CustomEvent", default: "—", description: "A run reaches its end, or <code>finish()</code> jumps there" },
          { name: "motion-cancel", type: "CustomEvent", default: "—", description: "<code>cancel()</code> stops a run and resets it" },
        ]}
      />
      <CodeWindow
        filename="app.js"
        copy
        code={`document.addEventListener('motion-finish', (event) => {
  console.log(event.target.localName, 'finished')
})`}
      />

      <h2>Package exports</h2>
      <PropsTable
        nameLabel="Export"
        rows={[
          { name: "pauseAll(root?)", type: "(root?: Node) => void", default: "document", description: "Pause running instances inside root; disable input-reactive components" },
          { name: "resumeAll(root?)", type: "(root?: Node) => void", default: "document", description: "Resume paused instances; re-enable only what pauseAll disabled" },
          { name: "cancelAll(root?)", type: "(root?: Node) => void", default: "document", description: "Cancel every instance inside root" },
          { name: "MotionControllable", type: "interface", default: "—", description: "TypeScript type for controllable element refs" },
          { name: "PlaybackState", type: "type", default: "—", description: "The four-state playback union" },
        ]}
      />

      <h2>Scroll-driven components</h2>
      <p>For <code>motion-parallax</code>, <code>motion-scene</code>, <code>motion-progress</code>, and <code>motion-blur</code>, the verbs adapt because these components track scroll position instead of a timeline. <code>pause()</code> unbinds the scroll listener and freezes the effect at its current progress. <code>play()</code> rebinds. <code>finish()</code> applies the end-of-scroll state. <code>cancel()</code> unbinds and resets styles. While bound, <code>playState</code> is <code>'running'</code>.</p>

      <h2>Accessibility</h2>
      <p>The API always respects <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>. A programmatic <code>play()</code> applies the final state instantly, still resolves <code>finished</code>, and still fires <code>motion-start</code> and <code>motion-finish</code>. So calling code never needs a reduced-motion branch. Two components keep running instead, because their job is ongoing: <code>motion-words</code> keeps changing words and <code>motion-countdown</code> keeps counting, both without animation, and the playback methods control them as usual.</p>
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script>
  import "motion-components/motion-circle";
  import "motion-components/motion-reveal";
  import "motion-components/motion-split";
  import "motion-components/motion-ticker";
  import "motion-components/motion-typewriter";
</script>

<style>
  .demo-card {
    background: var(--color-surface-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 2rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-text);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .loop-row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-around;
    gap: 2rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
